        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            10. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Spraying a Non-Selective Product on the Lawn</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A total weed and grass killer cannot tell dandelions from bluegrass. Owners trying to spot treat a lawn with one usually end up with a polka-dot pattern of dead circles that take a full season to fill in. If the weeds are in turf you want to keep, buy a selective lawn product labeled for your grass type and leave the non-selective jug for beds and edges.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Mixing Stronger Than the Label Says</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Doubling the rate feels like insurance, but it is a label violation and it often backfires. Some systemic sprays scorch leaves so quickly at high concentration that the chemical never reaches the roots, so the weed regrows. Extra product also means more runoff when it rains and more risk to nearby plants. Measure carefully and use the rate listed for your specific weed.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Spraying Pavement Instead of Weeds</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Coating an entire driveway or patio to catch a few cracks sends most of the product to the storm drain with the next rain. Hard surfaces do not absorb herbicide, so it sits on top and washes away. Aim the nozzle at the weed itself, use a coarse spray close to the crack, and treat only what is green.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using a Residual Killer Near Trees</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Soil-active vegetation killers stay in the ground for months, and tree roots spread far beyond the canopy, often beneath driveways and walks. Applying a residual product along a drive lined with maples or a hedge can cause leaf yellowing and dieback a season later. Use a non-residual product anywhere roots of plants you value might reach.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Letting Pets Back Out Too Soon</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Most labels say to keep people and pets off treated areas until the spray has dried, and granular products often must be watered in first. Letting the dog out to run through wet spray means chemical on paws that get licked clean. Plan applications for a dry morning when pets can stay inside for several hours, and store containers out of reach.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

