        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Which herbicide should I use on weeds in my grass?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For cool-season lawns, a selective product such as Tenacity or a three-way broadleaf herbicide labeled for your grass is the right tool, because it kills weeds while sparing turf. Check the label's grass list first, since some products injure bermudagrass, St. Augustine or centipede. Never use a non-selective glyphosate product on a lawn you want to keep, because it kills grass as readily as weeds.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How soon will I see sprayed weeds die?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Contact sprays and horticultural vinegar show wilting within hours on young weeds in warm sun. Systemic products such as glyphosate take longer, usually showing yellowing in several days and a full kill in one to two weeks, faster in warm weather and slower in cool. Selective lawn products often need two to three weeks and sometimes a second application to finish established perennial weeds.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is glyphosate safe to use around my home?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                The EPA's position is that glyphosate is not likely to cause cancer when used according to the label, while the World Health Organization's cancer research agency classified it as probably carcinogenic in 2015, and EPA's human health review is being redone after a 2022 court ruling. Practically, follow the label, wear gloves and eye protection, avoid drift, and keep people and pets off sprayed areas until dry.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long should pets stay off a treated lawn?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Follow the label on your specific product. For most liquid herbicides it says to keep people and pets out of the treated area until the spray has dried, which often takes a few hours on a warm, dry day. Granular products frequently need to be watered in first. When in doubt, wait longer, keep pets on a lead away from treated edges, and store containers securely.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Will rain wash away weed killer?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It can if the rain arrives before the product is rainfast. Labels list a rainfast period, often ranging from under an hour to several hours after the spray dries on leaves. Rain before then dilutes the dose on the plant and can carry product off pavement into storm drains. Check the forecast and spray on a dry morning with no rain expected for the rest of the day.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What kills weeds permanently?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                No product keeps ground weed-free forever. Systemic herbicides kill existing perennials to the roots, and soil-residual vegetation killers prevent new growth for months, but new seeds blow in and germinate eventually. The most durable results come from combining a root kill with prevention: mulch in beds, landscape fabric under gravel, dense healthy turf in lawns, and a pre-emergent where it is labeled.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does vinegar work as a weed killer?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Horticultural vinegar at 20% acetic acid burns down the leaves of young annual weeds quickly, especially in sunny, warm conditions. It does not move into roots, so established perennials such as dandelions usually grow back. Treat it with care: at that strength the label warns of serious eye damage, so goggles and gloves are required. Household 5% vinegar is much weaker and works only on tiny seedlings.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

